RecordSelect
============

(c) 2007 - 2016 Sergio Cambra, Lance Ivy

RecordSelect is a Rails widget to help you pick one record out of many. I designed it as a more usable and performant alternative to generating a massive dropdown list. It relies on AJAX, powered by jQuery, so jQuery must be loaded first, either from jquery-rails gem or just loading the JS in the assets pipeline, or imported before importing record_select if using importmap.

Please see the ActionView::Helpers::RecordSelectHelpers for the most common API. More documentation (and HOWTOs) can be found online at the wiki.

= USAGE WITH ASSETS PIPELINE

Add to application.js

```js
//= require jquery
//= require record_select
```

= USAGE WITH IMPORTMAP

Add to importmap.rb

```rb
pin "jquery"
pin "record_select"
```

Add to app/assets/config/manifest.js

```js
//= link jquery.js
//= link record_select.js
```

Add to app/javascript/application.js

```js
import 'jquery'
import 'record_select'
```

= USAGE WITH ACTIVE_SCAFFOLD

Either using assets pipeline or importmap, it will be loaded when requiring or importing active_scaffold, so no need to add it to application.js, importmap.rb or manifest.js

= CONTROLLER CONFIGURATION

Every controller must explicitly supply +search_on+. There is no automatic search across model columns:

```rb
record_select search_on: [:name, :email]
```

Use +search_on: []+ for a custom search implementation or when assigning search fields later. With an empty array, the built-in text search adds no conditions.

URL parameters matching model columns can also filter the records. Limit these separately from text search using global defaults, for example in an initializer:

```rb
RecordSelect.allowed_columns = [:id, :company_id]
RecordSelect.excluded_columns = [:password_digest, :reset_password_token]
```

Global defaults are captured and normalized when each controller calls +record_select+. Set them before controller configuration; later global changes do not affect existing configurations.

Override either default for a controller:

```rb
record_select search_on: [:name], allowed_columns: [:company_id, :active], excluded_columns: [:active]
```

Exclusions take precedence over allowed columns. Symbols and strings are accepted. Both +allowed_columns+ and +excluded_columns+ default to +[]+, disabling automatic URL column filters without requiring explicit configuration. An explicit +excluded_columns: nil+ is treated as +[]+ and clears any global exclusions for that controller; it does not enable filtering by itself.

To enable only specific filters for a controller:

```rb
record_select search_on: [:name], allowed_columns: [:company_id], excluded_columns: []
```

To explicitly restore unrestricted legacy filtering, use +allowed_columns: nil, excluded_columns: []+ (or the corresponding global settings). This permits filtering on hidden model columns and restores the reported disclosure risk. Prefer a narrow allowlist.

Controller options replace the corresponding global list. These settings do not restrict the trusted SQL expressions in +search_on+ or custom controller conditions.

= CALLBACKS

Register callbacks in a JavaScript file and pass their names to the helpers:

```js
RecordSelect.registerCallback('application.recordSelected', function(id, value, text, item) {
  // `this` is the RecordSelect widget.
});
```

```rb
link_to_record_select 'Select', 'users', onselect: 'application.recordSelected'
record_select_field 'user_id', user, rs: {onchange: 'application.recordSelected'}
```

JavaScript source strings are no longer accepted for +onselect+ or +onchange+. JavaScript callers may still pass a function directly.

= DEPENDENCIES
This depends on the excellent Paginator gem by Bruce Williams. This simple gem is available at paginator.rubyforge.org.
